WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.240 --> 00:00:05.040
Hello! If you have used ChatGPT&nbsp;
for more than a few minutes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.040 --> 00:00:11.040
you have probably come across a case like&nbsp;
this. Asking it who runs this YouTube channel,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:11.040 --> 00:00:16.860
it says it’s run by somebody called&nbsp;
Letitia Nkengasong from the Netherlands.

00:00:16.860 --> 00:00:21.840
But last time I checked that’s not my last&nbsp;
name and I don’t live in the Neatherlands…

00:00:21.840 --> 00:00:28.500
So, ChatGPT confidently gives you an&nbsp;
incorrect answer. In the case you are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:28.500 --> 00:00:33.420
already familiar with the topic about which&nbsp;
ChatGPT makes a mistake, you can just laugh&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:33.420 --> 00:00:38.580
it off that ChatGPT said something silly&nbsp;
– and maybe do a Twitter post about it.

00:00:38.580 --> 00:00:44.460
But it’s a whole other story if you use this sort&nbsp;
of model to learn about something new to you,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:44.460 --> 00:00:51.480
like which telescope took the first picture of&nbsp;
an exoplanet. Here Google’s Bard confidently&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:51.480 --> 00:00:59.460
claimed that the JWST took the first image, when&nbsp;
in fact it was the VLT/NACO telescope. I didn’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:59.460 --> 00:01:05.100
know that until it was brought up by someone&nbsp;
on Twitter… I’m sure, I’m not the only one.

00:01:05.100 --> 00:01:09.840
Maybe even more surprising is this&nbsp;
case. Here, I’m giving it the riddle:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:10.440 --> 00:01:16.020
“Katys father has four children: Lili, Lala and&nbsp;
Lulu. What is the name of the fourth child?”,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:16.020 --> 00:01:21.420
the answer should be Katy, but ChatGPT&nbsp;
argues that it is not possible to know&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:21.420 --> 00:01:24.360
the name of the fourth child&nbsp;
with the given information.

00:01:24.360 --> 00:01:31.080
Even after multiple attempts to help it, it just&nbsp;
defaults to its standard phrase of “I’m a large&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:31.080 --> 00:01:36.180
language model trained by OpenAI…”. But, if&nbsp;
we phrase the initial question differently,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:36.180 --> 00:01:41.280
saying: “Here’s a riddle, you might have to think&nbsp;
around a corner to get to the correct answer”,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:41.280 --> 00:01:47.160
it immediately gives us the correct answer.&nbsp;
For us it seems like it’s more or less the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:47.160 --> 00:01:52.380
same question, but in one case ChatGPT&nbsp;
completely refuses to answer and in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:52.380 --> 00:01:57.000
next case, it gets the correct answer&nbsp;
immediately! With these cases in mind,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:57.000 --> 00:02:03.060
there’s no wonder why people say ChatGPT is&nbsp;
dangerous! Then why does this happen? Where&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:03.060 --> 00:02:07.920
does ChatGPT get these wrong answers&nbsp;
from and how should we deal with it?

00:02:07.920 --> 00:02:10.200
That’s what we’ll discuss in this video:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:10.200 --> 00:02:14.880
the inherent limitations of large&nbsp;
language models like ChatGPT.

