00:03:38.580 --> 00:03:42.420
Great, now back to the video. It’s&nbsp;
clear that language models have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:42.420 --> 00:03:45.600
come a long way. Today’s large language models,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:45.600 --> 00:03:51.180
like ChatGPT can produce coherent text that&nbsp;
can read like it was written by a human.

00:03:51.180 --> 00:03:56.280
But the core idea of language modelling has&nbsp;
not changed: it is all a fancy autocomplete.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:56.280 --> 00:04:01.740
We are predicting a next plausible word&nbsp;
from previous other words. For ChatGPT,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:01.740 --> 00:04:06.480
these previous words are a so-called “prompt”,&nbsp;
which is basically a description given by the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:06.480 --> 00:04:09.420
programmers of the persona that&nbsp;
the language model will take.

00:04:09.420 --> 00:04:14.220
This prompt increases the probability that the&nbsp;
next words the language model will spit out,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:14.220 --> 00:04:19.740
are in line with this persona, in this&nbsp;
case a conversational AI, a chatbot. So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:19.740 --> 00:04:24.720
if here in the prompt we have the word “helpful”,&nbsp;
then most likely, the model will predict next&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:24.720 --> 00:04:30.120
words that match this description and will&nbsp;
not start using swear words – as easily.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:30.120 --> 00:04:32.940
Ok, so how does this language model converse?

00:04:32.940 --> 00:04:39.660
After this input prompt, which is hidden from you&nbsp;
in ChatGPT’s interface, it is your turn to ask it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:39.660 --> 00:04:45.300
a question. Then the language model autocompletes&nbsp;
by looking at the persona description and your&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:45.300 --> 00:04:49.440
question. And this autocompletion&nbsp;
will be the answer to your question.

00:04:49.440 --> 00:04:54.060
Then you say something again and it reads&nbsp;
the prompt, your first question, its answer,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:54.060 --> 00:04:58.860
your second question and based on this,&nbsp;
it continues to say likely words that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:58.860 --> 00:05:03.300
come after this whole history. And this&nbsp;
goes on and on, which each interaction&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:03.300 --> 00:05:08.160
from your side and the model’s response, you&nbsp;
make the model’s input, longer and longer.

00:05:08.160 --> 00:05:14.400
So, you see, ChatGPT is a fancy autocomplete&nbsp;
from a model that has read virtually the whole&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:14.400 --> 00:05:20.580
internet up to 2021 and has picked up patterns of&nbsp;
which words are likely to occur after previously&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:20.580 --> 00:05:27.060
given words. And it is important to highlight&nbsp;
that ChatGPT is giving you answers that “rhyme”&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:27.060 --> 00:05:33.600
given your answer and its training data, but&nbsp;
by no means does it do a “database lookup”.

00:05:33.600 --> 00:05:38.220
What it does is more like interpolating&nbsp;
points on a curve that was drawn between&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:38.220 --> 00:05:42.540
some measured data points. The measured&nbsp;
data points are the training data;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:42.540 --> 00:05:45.960
asking for something that is not&nbsp;
directly in the training data,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:45.960 --> 00:05:50.340
will give a result that is somewhere on&nbsp;
this curve, so the most likely answer.

00:05:50.340 --> 00:05:55.380
The way today’s large language models&nbsp;
are designed and trained leads to some&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:55.380 --> 00:06:00.600
limitations that are very important to&nbsp;
know and keep in mind when using them. So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:00.600 --> 00:06:03.480
let’s go over some of the most common ones:

00:06:03.480 --> 00:06:08.760
Knowing that ChatGPT is rather very good at&nbsp;
putting plausible words together, it is no&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:08.760 --> 00:06:14.520
wonder that it sometimes produces incorrect or&nbsp;
nonsensical answers. During training it does not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:14.520 --> 00:06:20.100
get feedback of what is true or untrue, especially&nbsp;
since the text on the internet that is used for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:20.100 --> 00:06:26.700
training is not always factually correct either.&nbsp;
ChatGPT and other similar models were trained&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:26.700 --> 00:06:32.160
in a process called reinforcement learning&nbsp;
from human feedback. Here the main training&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:32.160 --> 00:06:37.320
goal is to make the model’s output sound&nbsp;
plausible, not necessarily true or correct.

00:06:37.320 --> 00:06:43.140
When programmers train ChatGPT to be more cautious&nbsp;
when choosing to answer a question or not,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:43.920 --> 00:06:48.240
might make the model too cautious and it&nbsp;
declines to answer questions that it could&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:48.240 --> 00:06:52.800
have answered correctly. Even worse, when&nbsp;
the question from the user is ambiguous,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:52.800 --> 00:06:58.380
it does not ask for clarification and just guess&nbsp;
what the user intended. “Guessing what the user&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:58.380 --> 00:07:04.440
intended” is already a very anthropomorphizing&nbsp;
way to say that the language model autocompletes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:04.440 --> 00:07:10.380
anything with most plausible words. A follow-up&nbsp;
question asking for clarification is seldomly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:10.380 --> 00:07:16.080
the most plausible thing to say for the model,&nbsp;
given its training data. How often do people ask&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:16.080 --> 00:07:20.940
for clarification? They rather jump straight&nbsp;
into guessing what the interlocutor says.

00:07:20.940 --> 00:07:26.220
And speaking about what ChatGPT observed&nbsp;
or could not observe in the training data,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:26.220 --> 00:07:31.740
there is also the issue that human evaluators&nbsp;
and people generating training data have a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:31.740 --> 00:07:36.300
different knowledgebase than the model.&nbsp;
We humans have a lot of knowledge we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:36.300 --> 00:07:41.340
have accumulated in our lives and&nbsp;
is shared by our fellow humans.

00:07:41.340 --> 00:07:46.260
In our usual conversations, we tend to discuss&nbsp;
some things, which we consider common sense,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:46.260 --> 00:07:51.480
much less often than other things.&nbsp;
ChatGPT learns to clone the behavior&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:51.480 --> 00:07:56.820
it sees in human generated text examples.&nbsp;
Since human conversations do not mention&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:56.820 --> 00:08:02.640
basic and self-evident facts – for humans, but&nbsp;
not for a language model – this leads to a gap&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:02.640 --> 00:08:06.180
in the training data and therefore&nbsp;
in the knowledgebase of the model.

00:08:06.180 --> 00:08:09.000
For example: You will rarely find conversations&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:09.000 --> 00:08:12.780
about the underlying motivation to&nbsp;
bring an umbrella if it’s raining.&nbsp;

00:08:12.780 --> 00:08:19.200
When the model reads the conversation: “Why&nbsp;
did John get wet? – Because he didn’t have an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:19.200 --> 00:08:23.040
umbrella!”, without the knowledge of&nbsp;
what an umbrella does, the model can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:23.040 --> 00:08:27.300
incorrectly infer that not having an&nbsp;
umbrella generally leads to wetness.

00:08:27.900 --> 00:08:34.440
So, when asking the model later on, what one could&nbsp;
do not to get so wet when falling into the pool,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:34.440 --> 00:08:39.120
the model could suggest bringing an&nbsp;
umbrella. Even worse, it will phrase&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:39.120 --> 00:08:44.940
its answer confidently because it has previously&nbsp;
read confident answers, like the one seen before.

00:08:44.940 --> 00:08:49.980
This is of course a simplified example,&nbsp;
but it demonstrates how differing levels&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:49.980 --> 00:08:53.820
of knowledge while learning from&nbsp;
example conversations lead to the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:53.820 --> 00:08:57.600
so-called hallucination of facts, as&nbsp;
considering the model’s knowledgebase,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:57.600 --> 00:09:02.700
the answers in the examples seem just&nbsp;
as arbitrary as the answers it gives.

00:09:02.700 --> 00:09:07.320
Another problem with this extremely&nbsp;
complex ChatGPT “rhyme machine”,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:07.320 --> 00:09:11.340
is that sometimes a little change in&nbsp;
the wording of the input or even in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:11.340 --> 00:09:16.680
the punctuation of the input can elicit very&nbsp;
different answers from the model. Even weirder,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:16.680 --> 00:09:21.120
in one phrasing the answer is correct,&nbsp;
in another wording it might be incorrect!

00:09:21.120 --> 00:09:26.220
Even more interestingly, words and phrases can&nbsp;
be used to make ChatGPT ignore its previous&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:26.220 --> 00:09:33.240
prompt as designed by OpenAI. As OpenAI fixes&nbsp;
these jailbreaks in every new ChatGPT version,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:33.240 --> 00:09:38.580
it becomes harder and harder to make it become&nbsp;
harmful and make other statements against its&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:38.580 --> 00:09:43.740
guidelines. But it is not impossible to cause&nbsp;
jailbreaks even in the newest ChatGPT version.

00:09:43.740 --> 00:09:48.780
So, language models being language models&nbsp;
and rhyming their answer out of a question,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:48.780 --> 00:09:55.260
rather than checking facts, will continue to&nbsp;
produce wrong answers for now. Of course, there is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:55.260 --> 00:10:00.900
active research in fact checking and in creating&nbsp;
language models that can read sources, but we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:00.900 --> 00:10:06.600
see how that’s going for the new BingSearch.&nbsp;
BingSearch is a perfect example of how OpenAI&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:06.600 --> 00:10:11.640
gathered a lot of feedback from users through&nbsp;
ChatGPT and when they helped Microsoft release&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:11.640 --> 00:10:16.920
a more powerful version that can also look up on&nbsp;
the Internet, everything got even more complicated&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:16.920 --> 00:10:22.440
since BingSearch can now look up things now&nbsp;
that are sometimes correct, but sometimes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:22.440 --> 00:10:28.800
wrong and it has no way to discern between the&nbsp;
two. This leads to it still hallucinating facts&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:28.800 --> 00:10:34.020
and getting frustrated that the internet&nbsp;
already knows its secret codename Sydney.

00:10:34.020 --> 00:10:39.240
So, clearly, factual correctness in the output&nbsp;
of large language models is understudied,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:39.240 --> 00:10:44.760
but now with language models that can search&nbsp;
the internet, a whole new problem opens: so far,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:44.760 --> 00:10:49.380
language models cannot help themselves&nbsp;
to produce incorrect output. But now,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:49.380 --> 00:10:53.640
they also have to recognize that&nbsp;
there are wrong facts in their inputs!

00:10:53.640 --> 00:11:01.500
Does this mean chatGPT and co. are completely&nbsp;
useless today? No, not at all. These models can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:01.500 --> 00:11:06.960
be extremely helpful for helping you write nice&nbsp;
texts, but you must keep these limitations in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:06.960 --> 00:11:16.620
mind, be able to correct the mistakes yourself.&nbsp;
And you should not use them for looking up facts.&nbsp;

00:11:16.620 --> 00:11:20.280
We are curious to see the future&nbsp;
developments of this technology.

00:11:21.060 --> 00:11:24.300
As always, we will keep you updated&nbsp;
when something interesting comes.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:24.300 --> 00:11:28.511
We hope you liked this video and that&nbsp;
we will see you next time. Okay, bye!

